<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.8.3: Understanding That the Quadratic Formula Is the Combined Steps of Completing the Square</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02466</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.8.3: Understanding That the Quadratic Formula Is the Combined Steps of Completing the Square</md:title>
    <md:uuid>cd6127d6-3270-415d-9f0e-0a7d5f52250d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Here is one solution for solving \(x^2+3x+8=0\) by <term data-schema-version="“1.0”" data-store="glossary-tooltip">completing the square</term>, where each step is shown, but numerical expressions are not evaluated.</para>
    <para>Original equation
      \(x^2+3x+8=0\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - \(x^2+3x=-8\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - \(4x^2+4(3x)=4(-8)\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - \((2x)^2+6(2x)=-32\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - \(P^2+6P=-32\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - \(P^2+6P+3^2=-32+3^2\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - \((P+3)^2=3^2-32\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - \(P+3= \pm \sqrt{3^2-32}\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 8</emphasis> - \(P=-3 \pm \sqrt{3^2-32}\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 9</emphasis> - \(2x=-3 \pm \sqrt{3^2-32}\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 10</emphasis> - 
      \(x= \frac{-3 \pm \sqrt{3^2-32}}{2}\)</para>
    <para>In Step 2, the equation is multiplied by 4. Why might that be?</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>To make the coefficient of \(x^2\) a <term data-schema-version="“1.0”" data-store="glossary-tooltip">perfect square</term> and the coefficient of \(x\) an even number</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>In Step 5, \(3^2\) is added to each side. Why might that be?</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(3^2\) completes the square for the expression on the left side. To keep the two sides equal, it needs to be added to the other side as well.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>What happened between Step 5 and Step 6?</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>The perfect square is rewritten in factored form, and \(-32+3^2\) is rewritten as \(3^2-32\).</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<div class="os-raise-student-reflection">
  <para class="os-raise-student-reflection-title">Building Character: Intellectual Humility</para>
  <div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-gap-1">
    <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/0943d627452fc6cc10570e6cded146178d40e44d"/></media></figure><div>
      <para class="os-raise-mb-0">No matter how old you are, with intellectual humility you become wiser. It helps you be less judgmental of others, learn more in school, and be a better leader. </para>
      <para>Think about yourself. Are these statements true for you?</para>
      <list>
        <item>
          I recognize the value in opinions that are different from my own. 
        </item>
        <item>
          I accept that my beliefs and attitudes may be wrong. 
        </item>
      </list>
      <para>Don’t worry if none of these statements are true for you. Developing this trait takes time. Your first step starts today!</para>
     </div>
  </div>
</div></section>
</content>
</document>
